>>
endobj
771 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 608 0 R
/T (Coalition sanctions have denied Russia an estimated $450-500 billion since February 2022. Russian fossil fuel export revenues fell to 464 million euros per day in January 2026, the lowest since the full-scale invasion, as the EU's dynamic price cap, October 2025 OFAC designations of Rosneft and Lukoil, and Urals crude discounts above $25 per barrel compressed margins simultaneously.1 These costs remain suboptimal. CREA analysis indicates that enforcement at a $30 per barrel threshold would have denied an additional 162 billion euros from December 2022 through November 2025; the distance between actual and achievable denial defines the scope of unrealized leverage.2)
/E (Coalition sanctions have denied Russia an estimated $450-500 billion since February 2022. Russian fossil fuel export revenues fell to 464 million euros per day in January 2026, the lowest since the full-scale invasion, as the EU's dynamic price cap, October 2025 OFAC designations of Rosneft and Lukoil, and Urals crude discounts above $25 per barrel compressed margins simultaneously.1 These costs remain suboptimal. CREA analysis indicates that enforcement at a $30 per barrel threshold would have denied an additional 162 billion euros from December 2022 through November 2025; the distance between actual and achievable denial defines the scope of unrealized leverage.2)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [25 27 26 28]
>>
endobj
772 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 608 0 R
/T (Shadow fleet operations constitute the primary erosion mechanism: by November 2025, shadow tankers carried 72 percent of Russian seaborne crude exports, and the fleet reconstitutes faster than designation campaigns can shrink it.3 The shift toward active interdiction through Operation Southern Spear signals a toughened posture but has not closed the structural gap between designated and operational shadow capacity.4)
/E (Shadow fleet operations constitute the primary erosion mechanism: by November 2025, shadow tankers carried 72 percent of Russian seaborne crude exports, and the fleet reconstitutes faster than designation campaigns can shrink it.3 The shift toward active interdiction through Operation Southern Spear signals a toughened posture but has not closed the structural gap between designated and operational shadow capacity.4)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [29 31 30 32]
>>
endobj
773 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 608 0 R
/T (Secondary sanctions on India and China, which together absorb approximately 85 percent of Russian seaborne crude, represent the largest unrealized leverage. The October 2025 designations produced measurable behavioral change: India's Russian crude intake fell 29 percent month-on-month by December, approximately 150 million barrels of Russian crude remained without buyers at sea by February 2026, and Rystad Energy projects Russia may need to cut production by 300,000 barrels per day by spring 2026.5)
/E (Secondary sanctions on India and China, which together absorb approximately 85 percent of Russian seaborne crude, represent the largest unrealized leverage. The October 2025 designations produced measurable behavioral change: India's Russian crude intake fell 29 percent month-on-month by December, approximately 150 million barrels of Russian crude remained without buyers at sea by February 2026, and Rystad Energy projects Russia may need to cut production by 300,000 barrels per day by spring 2026.5)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [33]
>>
