<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 617 0 R
/T (The Dallas Federal Reserve Working Paper by Kilian, Rapson, and Schipper \(revised January 2025\) finds that the embargo forced Russia to accept a $32 per barrel discount in March 2023, decomposed into approximately $15 from higher transport costs on rerouted shipments and $17 from increased buyer bargaining power as Russian sellers lost access to competitive European markets.35 The cap's direct effect on the FOB price, the authors find, proved "negligible" once it facilitated the EU's relaxation of the maritime services ban; the cap's contribution was institutional rather than price-mechanical. Spiro, Wachtmeister, and Gars reach a parallel conclusion in Energy Policy \(2025\): the embargo constituted "the most consequential sanction," and the cap functioned primarily "as a partial transport embargo."36 Catherine Wolfram, who helped design the cap as Treasury Deputy Assistant Secretary, disputes the "negligible" characterization and argues the cap's threat of service withdrawal influenced price formation even when not formally a constraint.37)
/E (The Dallas Federal Reserve Working Paper by Kilian, Rapson, and Schipper \(revised January 2025\) finds that the embargo forced Russia to accept a $32 per barrel discount in March 2023, decomposed into approximately $15 from higher transport costs on rerouted shipments and $17 from increased buyer bargaining power as Russian sellers lost access to competitive European markets.35 The cap's direct effect on the FOB price, the authors find, proved "negligible" once it facilitated the EU's relaxation of the maritime services ban; the cap's contribution was institutional rather than price-mechanical. Spiro, Wachtmeister, and Gars reach a parallel conclusion in Energy Policy \(2025\): the embargo constituted "the most consequential sanction," and the cap functioned primarily "as a partial transport embargo."36 Catherine Wolfram, who helped design the cap as Treasury Deputy Assistant Secretary, disputes the "negligible" characterization and argues the cap's threat of service withdrawal influenced price formation even when not formally a constraint.37)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [20 23 21 24 22 25]
>>
endobj
956 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 617 0 R
/T (The discount trajectory reveals a critical pattern. The spread narrowed steadily as the shadow fleet matured and contracted to $4-6 by August-September 2025, the smallest gap since the invasion. The October 2025 designations reversed this trend within weeks: the discount spiked to $23-24 in November, widened to $26-27 in December, and reached $29.20 in January 2026.38 The mechanism matters for the leverage argument: if the embargo drives the structural discount and the cap contributes primarily through its enforcement architecture, then the enforcement gap, the failure to enforce service restrictions against the shadow fleet, is the key variable. Tighter enforcement amplifies the price effect without additional sanctions legislation.)
/E (The discount trajectory reveals a critical pattern. The spread narrowed steadily as the shadow fleet matured and contracted to $4-6 by August-September 2025, the smallest gap since the invasion. The October 2025 designations reversed this trend within weeks: the discount spiked to $23-24 in November, widened to $26-27 in December, and reached $29.20 in January 2026.38 The mechanism matters for the leverage argument: if the embargo drives the structural discount and the cap contributes primarily through its enforcement architecture, then the enforcement gap, the failure to enforce service restrictions against the shadow fleet, is the key variable. Tighter enforcement amplifies the price effect without additional sanctions legislation.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [26 28 27]
>>
