/T (Three cumulative denial metrics, each at a different scope, define the distance between the architecture's actual and potential economic impact. The broadest, total sanctions denial since February 2022, ranges from $450 to $500 billion, of which approximately $285 billion represents immobilized Central Bank reserves; revenue-only denial therefore falls at roughly $165-215 billion.39 A narrower metric captures the price cap compliance gap: CREA estimates that full enforcement of the original $60 cap from December 2022 through mid-2025 would have denied an additional approximately 39.5 billion euros \($45 billion\) beyond what the architecture actually captured.40 The most analytically significant metric is CREA's $30 per barrel counterfactual: enforcement at $30, still above Russia's average production cost of $15, would have denied 162 billion euros cumulatively from December 2022 through November 2025, equivalent to 39 percent of Russian oil export revenue over that period.41)
/E (Three cumulative denial metrics, each at a different scope, define the distance between the architecture's actual and potential economic impact. The broadest, total sanctions denial since February 2022, ranges from $450 to $500 billion, of which approximately $285 billion represents immobilized Central Bank reserves; revenue-only denial therefore falls at roughly $165-215 billion.39 A narrower metric captures the price cap compliance gap: CREA estimates that full enforcement of the original $60 cap from December 2022 through mid-2025 would have denied an additional approximately 39.5 billion euros \($45 billion\) beyond what the architecture actually captured.40 The most analytically significant metric is CREA's $30 per barrel counterfactual: enforcement at $30, still above Russia's average production cost of $15, would have denied 162 billion euros cumulatively from December 2022 through November 2025, equivalent to 39 percent of Russian oil export revenue over that period.41)
