/T (The current global oil surplus, with the IEA's projection of approximately 3.8 mbd for 2026, substantially mitigates the price-spike risk that constrained 2022 policy. Brooks and Harris argue that sanctions are superior to tariffs precisely because they "can be scaled up and down incrementally, which gives U.S. policymakers a degree of control they would not have with 100 or 500% tariffs."79 The October action demonstrated that targeted deployment produces measurable behavioral change without worst-case economic or diplomatic consequences. Secondary sanctions represent the largest unrealized lever in the sanctions architecture; the question  is how to sequence escalation, against which targets, at what pace, and tied to what negotiating objectives.)
/E (The current global oil surplus, with the IEA's projection of approximately 3.8 mbd for 2026, substantially mitigates the price-spike risk that constrained 2022 policy. Brooks and Harris argue that sanctions are superior to tariffs precisely because they "can be scaled up and down incrementally, which gives U.S. policymakers a degree of control they would not have with 100 or 500% tariffs."79 The October action demonstrated that targeted deployment produces measurable behavioral change without worst-case economic or diplomatic consequences. Secondary sanctions represent the largest unrealized lever in the sanctions architecture; the question  is how to sequence escalation, against which targets, at what pace, and tied to what negotiating objectives.)
