/T (Russia's domestic indicators suggest that sanctions pressure compounds rather than plateaus. The National Wealth Fund's liquid assets fell from approximately $113.5 billion before the invasion to $51.8 billion by November 2025; Gazprombank analysts estimate that at $30-35 per barrel oil, remaining funds deplete by end of 2026.91 GDP growth fell from 4.3 percent in 2024 to an IMF forecast of 0.8 percent for 2026. The Bank of Finland assesses that Russia "finds itself constrained to annual growth rates near its long-term potential rate of around 1 percent" and calls "recession a very real possibility."92 The Bank of Russia cut the key rate six consecutive times to 15.5 percent from a peak of 21 percent, consistent with decelerating activity. Record-low unemployment of 2.1 percent reflects labor shortages, approximately 1.5 million people lost to war casualties and emigration, not economic strength.93)
/E (Russia's domestic indicators suggest that sanctions pressure compounds rather than plateaus. The National Wealth Fund's liquid assets fell from approximately $113.5 billion before the invasion to $51.8 billion by November 2025; Gazprombank analysts estimate that at $30-35 per barrel oil, remaining funds deplete by end of 2026.91 GDP growth fell from 4.3 percent in 2024 to an IMF forecast of 0.8 percent for 2026. The Bank of Finland assesses that Russia "finds itself constrained to annual growth rates near its long-term potential rate of around 1 percent" and calls "recession a very real possibility."92 The Bank of Russia cut the key rate six consecutive times to 15.5 percent from a peak of 21 percent, consistent with decelerating activity. Record-low unemployment of 2.1 percent reflects labor shortages, approximately 1.5 million people lost to war casualties and emigration, not economic strength.93)
