/Title (Sanctions relief constitutes a necessary settlement component. Oil and gas revenues fell below 23 percent of Russia's federal budget in 2025, yet fiscal pressure continues to compound: the Bank of Russia cut its key rate six consecutive times to 15.5 percent from a 21 percent peak, GDP growth decelerated from 4.1 percent in 2024 to approximately 1 percent in 2025, and KSE Institute projects oil export revenues at $106 billion in 2026, down from $189 billion in 2024.6 The draft peace plan proposes sanctions removal "in stages and on a case-by-case basis" with snapback provisions, a marked retreat from Moscow's demand for immediate unconditional removal. The JCPOA precedent and the Atlantic Council's eleven guiding principles, with benchmarks tied to child returns, POW releases, and withdrawal milestones, provide actionable frameworks for phased relief.7)
