/Title (The Presidential Special Envoy's Office must determine how to sequence enforcement escalation, secondary sanctions deployment, and relief offers within the active Russia-Ukraine negotiating framework. The G7 oil price cap and broader energy sanctions regime have compressed Russian fossil fuel revenues to their lowest level since the February 2022 full-scale invasion, yet CREA counterfactual analysis indicates that enforcement at a $30 per barrel threshold would have denied Russia an additional 162 billion euros in cumulative oil export revenue from December 2022 through November 2025, revenue that instead financed Moscow's war effort.9 This enforcement gap, the distance between actual and achievable revenue denial, defines the scope of unrealized leverage. Oil and gas revenues remained the single largest individual revenue category in the Russian federal budget in 2025, even as their share fell below 23 percent, a record low, and January 2026 oil and gas tax receipts fell 50.2 percent year-on-year.10 Miscalibration carries asymmetric risk: premature relief forfeits leverage that the coalition cannot reconstitute once surrendered, while escalation that fractures the diplomatic process squanders a negotiating window in which three rounds of trilateral talks have narrowed outstanding issues to territorial terms.11 Multiple convergent factors, including the EU's proposed replacement of the price cap with a full maritime services ban, measurable buyer behavioral change from the October 2025 Rosneft and Lukoil designations, sharp Russian fiscal deterioration, and a reported June 2026 target for a settlement framework, compress the decision timeline into the first half of 2026.12)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3447 0 R
/Next 3450 0 R
>>
endobj
3450 0 obj
<<
