/Title (A Treasury team developed the cap as a compromise: maintain Russian crude flows to global markets but cap the revenue Moscow could extract per barrel. The G7 endorsed the concept at Elmau \(June 2022\), and the cap took effect at $60 on December 5, 2022. The $60 level reflected political calibration rather than analytical optimum; Russia's marginal production cost averaged $10-20 per barrel and its fiscal breakeven hovered near $40, but European winter energy concerns pushed the coalition toward a conservative level at which Urals crude already traded.)
