/Title (The January 10, 2025, OFAC action shifted the architecture from indirect service denial to direct entity designation. In one of the Biden administration's final sanctions actions, OFAC designated 183 oil-carrying vessels and placed Gazprom Neft and Surgutneftegas on the SDN list.26 The October 22, 2025, designation of Rosneft and Lukoil constituted the most consequential single action in the architecture's history: the two companies together produce approximately 55 percent of Russia's oil and contribute roughly 25 percent of federal revenue.27 Combined with prior designations, more than 75 percent of Russian oil exports now fall under U.S. sanctions. SDN designation operates through a different coercive mechanism than the price cap: it prohibits any transaction with the designated entity by U.S. persons and creates secondary sanctions exposure for non-U.S. persons who continue to deal with Rosneft or Lukoil. Treasury explicitly framed the action as coercive; the stated objective of sanctions is "not to punish, but to bring about a positive change in behavior."28 The gap between designated and undesignated producers, notably Tatneft \(approximately 565,000 barrels per day, CEO personally sanctioned but company undesignated\), represents additional unused escalation authority available to OFAC.)
