/Title (Russian fossil fuel export revenues declined 54 percent from their post-invasion peak, from approximately 1,000 million euros per day in March 2022 to 464 million euros per day in January 2026, the lowest level since the full-scale invasion.32 The decline occurred in four distinct phases. The first \(February-November 2022\) produced a counterintuitive surge as commodity price spikes pushed revenues to approximately 868 million euros per day, and Russia earned 158 billion euros before the cap took effect. The second \(December 2022-mid-2023\) captured the cap's initial impact: revenues dropped to 640-700 million euros per day then stabilized as the shadow fleet scaled and attestation fraud spread. The third \(mid-2023-mid-2025\) traced a sustained but slow decline through the 600s, with the January 2025 OFAC designations an accelerant.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3481 0 R
>>
endobj
3486 0 obj
<<
/Title (The fourth phase \(September 2025-January 2026\) produced the sharpest compression and carries direct implications for the leverage assessment. The floating cap, Rosneft and Lukoil designations, and secondary sanctions effects combined to drive revenues from 546 million euros per day in September to 464 in January, a four-month decline that exceeded the previous year's total erosion. The IEA's February 2026 Oil Market Report independently characterized Russian oil revenues as "roughly half pre-invasion levels."33)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3476 0 R
/Prev 3481 0 R
/Next 3482 0 R
>>
endobj
3487 0 obj
