/Title (The quantitative evidence supports a calibrated assessment: the sanctions architecture works but operates well below its design potential. The Bank of Finland, among the most authoritative independent monitors of the Russian economy, published a January 2026 analysis with a section titled "Sanctions have worked": Urals-Brent discounts widened from $1-2 to over $20; the National Wealth Fund's liquid assets depleted; GDP growth constrained to approximately 1 percent; Gazprom posted net losses of $7 billion \(2023\) and $12.89 billion \(2024\).45 The GAO, through synthetic control methodology, found Russia's 2022 growth was approximately 6 percentage points below a no-sanctions counterfactual.46 CSIS characterized the Russian economy as "down but not out" and the sanctions toolkit as "among the most potent weapons to contain Russian power."47)
