/Title (The shadow fleet constitutes the single largest obstacle to sanctions effectiveness. No consensus definition exists: estimates range from 435 vessels \(KSE Institute, Russia-specific\) to approximately 980 \(S&P Global, combined Russia-Iran-Venezuela fleet\), and the EU designated approximately 598 by December 2025.52 The operationally relevant metric, however, is not vessel counts but the share of Russian crude that moves outside Western services. CREA's November 2025 data found that sanctioned shadow tankers carried 44 percent, unsanctioned shadow vessels carried 26 percent, and G7-coalition vessels carried 30 percent; approximately 70 percent of Russian seaborne crude therefore moves outside the sanctions architecture's intended control.53)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3511 0 R
/Next 3516 0 R
>>
endobj
3516 0 obj
<<
/Title (The fleet's physical and regulatory characteristics create concrete enforcement levers. KSE data indicate that 86 percent of shadow fleet tankers exceeded 15 years of age by September 2025, a figure that Gibson Shipbrokers assessed had risen to 93-94 percent by early 2026.54 Only 21 percent of Russian crude moved on tankers insured by International Group P&I clubs; non-IG alternatives consist primarily of Russian state insurers whose policies likely contain exclusion clauses that void coverage for cargoes in breach of sanctions \(KSE\).55 Flag-state concentration compounds the vulnerability: Panama and Gabon together account for approximately 42 percent of shadow fleet registrations, and flag changes remain endemic; CREA documented 134 sanctioned vessels that shifted registries within three months of designation.56)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3511 0 R
/Prev 3515 0 R
>>
endobj
3517 0 obj
