/Title (Environmental liability has mobilized enforcement action that purely sanctions-focused arguments did not achieve. The Cook Islands-flagged Eagle S severed the Estlink 2 power cable and four data cables in the Gulf of Finland in December 2024; Finnish forces boarded the vessel, found 32 faults, and deemed it unseaworthy, an incident that shifted the shadow fleet threat from environmental to hybrid-infrastructure risk.59 The liability gap magnifies the exposure: the IG P&I system covers up to $1 billion for oil pollution, but shadow fleet vessels fall outside this framework, and CREA estimates cleanup costs for a major spill could reach $1.6 billion.60 Denmark's port-state inspections, the nine-nation demand for insurance documentation from Russian-linked vessels, and Germany's seizure of the Eventin all originated from environmental and safety concerns rather than sanctions compliance.61 This pattern suggests that enforcement framed as environmental protection, rather than sanctions compliance alone, expands the coalition of willing enforcers, particularly among Nordic and Baltic states with the geographic exposure and political will to act.)
