/Title (The decisive question for enforcement strategy is whether designation campaigns can shrink the shadow fleet faster than Russia reconstitutes it. KSE data show that designated tankers that continued to load crude in Russia grew from 44 in January 2025 to 143 by November 2025; simultaneously, new unsanctioned vessels entered service.62 Despite 621 designations across jurisdictions, Baltic crude exports set an all-time record in January 2026 at 12.7 million tonnes. Craig Kennedy \(Harvard Davis Center\) argued in May 2025 that concentrated designation rounds had achieved a 46 percent capacity reduction, but the data since May indicate the reduction proved temporary as the fleet recovered through continued tanker acquisitions.63 Robin Brooks \(Brookings\) identified the structural asymmetry that explains this resilience: Indian ports reject OFAC-sanctioned vessels but accept EU- and UK-sanctioned ones, and OFAC sanctions achieve approximately 70 percent productivity reduction versus approximately 30 percent for EU-only designations.64)
/Dest [33 0 R /Fit]
