/Title (The sanctions architecture targets the supply side of Russian oil exports through service restrictions; secondary sanctions target the demand side by alteration of buyer behavior. The October 2025 Rosneft and Lukoil designations demonstrated this mechanism's power: India's Russian crude imports fell 29 percent month-on-month, approximately 150 million barrels of Russian oil accumulated at sea by February 2026, and Rystad Energy projected Russia may need to cut production by 300,000 barrels per day by spring 2026.66 The mechanism operates through the centrality of the U.S. dollar system. Edward Fishman \(Columbia CGEP\) frames the asymmetry: the dollar features in approximately 90 percent of global foreign exchange transactions, whereas the United States accounts for only 13 percent of global imports; secondary sanctions leverage the financial system's reach far beyond the direct trade relationship.67 Kpler data quantify the differential: OFAC sanctions produce an average 70 percent decline in sanctioned vessel productivity; EU and UK sanctions produce only 30 percent, because India accepts EU- and UK-sanctioned vessels but rejects OFAC-sanctioned ones.)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3529 0 R
>>
endobj
