/Title (Three structural factors explain the sharpness of India's adjustment. Dollar dependence in India's banking system and major refiners' U.S. capital market exposure created immediate compliance incentives, compounded by the bilateral tariff lever \(50 percent tariff, with Treasury Secretary Bessent's characterization of India's import surge as "profiteering"\).71 India's historical compliance pattern, with New Delhi's previous halt of Iranian and Venezuelan crude under U.S. pressure, established a baseline of responsiveness. India's refining-export nexus created an additional EU-facing vulnerability: Indian refiners exported 61.2 million barrels of Russian-crude-derived products to Europe in 2025, compared to 1.8 million from China.72 The Nayara exception reveals a deliberate architectural gap. Rosneft holds 49.13 percent of Nayara Energy, structured precisely below OFAC's 50 percent blocking threshold. Nayara doubled its Russian crude intake to approximately 471,000 bpd by January 2026, 40 percent of Russia's remaining India shipments, and new intermediaries \(RusExport, MorExport, Zarubezhneft\) emerged within weeks, a reconstitution pattern that mirrors the January 2025 designation response.73)
/Dest [35 0 R /Fit]
