/Title (India imported approximately 1.75-1.80 million barrels per day of Russian crude in January through October 2025, which represented 35-40 percent of total crude imports, up from below 2.5 percent before the invasion.68 After the October designations, imports collapsed to approximately 1.1 mbd by December, a three-year low; Russia's share of Indian imports fell from approximately 36 percent to 24-25 percent.69 Jamnagar, India's largest refinery complex, processed zero Russian crude in January 2026. By February, Saudi Arabia became India's primary crude supplier for the first time since the invasion. Reliance Industries halted Russian imports entirely; only Indian Oil Corporation and Nayara Energy secured December deliveries. The November pre-compliance surge, in which imports rose paradoxically to 1.80-1.85 mbd as refiners stockpiled ahead of the General License 126 wind-down, actually strengthened the proof of concept: refiners treated the sanctions as credible enough to justify accelerated purchases before the compliance deadline closed.70)
