/Title (The G7 price cap coalition has operated under two different caps since September 2025: the United States at $60 per barrel, unchanged since December 2022, and the EU at $44.10 as of February 2026. A transaction at $50 per barrel is compliant under U.S. rules and non-compliant under EU rules.80 The divergence crystallized at the G7 finance ministers' meeting in Banff in May 2025, where Treasury Secretary Bessent blocked a proposed cap reduction; the EU proceeded unilaterally through the 18th package floating formula, and Trade Commissioner Dombrovskis stated the EU would "not shy away from taking steps at the EU level."81 The consequence: the U.S. cap sits $22.40 above January 2026 Urals prices of $37.60, which renders it functionally non-binding and undermines the coalition's credibility at the moment enforcement tools have reached their greatest potential effectiveness.)
