/Title (The fourth action addresses the U.S.-EU enforcement divergence through explicit instrument-assignment rather than cap harmonization. The Banff deadlock produced a dual-cap system \($60 U.S. / $44.10 EU\) that undermines enforcement coherence. Rather than pursue a negotiation Bessent blocked, the Envoy should propose a functional division: the United States focuses on OFAC designations and secondary sanctions, where its dollar-system leverage produces a 70 percent vessel productivity decline; the EU focuses on floating cap calibration, the proposed maritime services ban, and SWIFT architecture.108 The Atlantic Council has documented this de facto division; formalization into an explicit coordination framework would resolve the gap without either party's abandonment of its preferred enforcement philosophy.)
